group: Cs
formula: CH4O
construction: Fragment orbitals (extended Hückel)
headline: 
note: Fragments: C1 against OH, H, H, H (extended Hückel, qualitative) · energies in Hartree · labels in C<sub>3v</sub> (local, approximate) · 1 level hidden (Show all levels)
about: **Where the fragment levels come from.** The left column is the valence orbitals of the picked C atom, the right column the orbitals of the pieces left when it is removed (OH, H, H, H), each piece neutral, from a quick extended Hückel calculation. Each is placed at the energy it has inside the molecule before it mixes with anything. This puts every bonding level below the two levels it comes from and every antibonding level above them, as in a textbook diagram. Treat their positions as qualitative.\n\n**How reliable each fragment level is: 64–94 %.** A fragment level can only be placed using the orbitals the calculation reported. The percentage says how much of the fragment orbital those orbitals cover. Levels below 90 % show their percentage next to them; their positions are less certain.\n\n**Lines.** A line joins a molecular orbital to a fragment orbital that makes up at least 10 % of it. Both members of a bonding/antibonding pair are joined to both of their parents.\n\n**Symmetry labels** are those of the local skeleton, C and its four bonded atoms (C<sub>3v</sub>), and are approximate: the rest of the molecule breaks that symmetry. Beside each orbital is how well it fits its label. A label is shown as it is when the fit is 90 % or more, with a ~ in front from 50 %, and left off below that.\n\n**Not shown:** 24 molecular orbitals that are less than half made of the fragments' valence orbitals. Nearly all of them are high, empty orbitals. 1 further level sits on the ligands alone or has little metal character; tick Show all levels to draw it. Also not shown: 2 core orbitals below −10.21 Hartree.\n\n**Total electrons: 14** (C 4, pieces 10).\n\n**Orbital labels.** Symmetry labels computed from the orbitals in Cs; the code reported none.\n\n**Picked atoms.** One atom is drawn against the atoms bonded to it; two bonded atoms cut that bond and the two sides are drawn against each other (in and out of phase when they are equal). A metal gives the fragment-orbital diagram, each ligand being a connected piece left when the metal is removed. A single atom that is not a metal is drawn the same way when it is not fixed by every symmetry operation of the molecule, or when atoms are bonded to neither it nor its neighbours: the atom against the connected pieces left when it is removed, each piece neutral.
